  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION/MARINE/BEACHES DISCUSSION...\n\nNighttime satellite shows patchy low clouds struggling to develop at \nthe coast with some high clouds above streaming in from the weak \nsubtropical jet. At the moment, there is nothing else remarkable on \nsatellite over our region. \n\nA few hundred miles southwest of the coast, two visible weather \nfeatures give us important context into the weather pattern this \nweekend into early next week. The stratocumulus deck is moving \nsouthwestward, as it does with northeasterly flow circulating around \nthe North Pacific surface high pressure. Meanwhile, high clouds are \nmoving the opposite direction - carried by the southwest winds of \nthe jet stream. The flow reveals a fairly stationary trough as the \nclouds flow through a stationary path. What cannot be seen on \nsatellite is the cyclonic flow at 850 and 700mb off the Baja coast \nwhich is beginning to advect an elevated but deep and moist airmass \nabove 850 mbs associated with Hurricane Polo and Hurricane Odalys. \nPWAT's will generally increase to 1-1.5\" tonight into Sunday, \nblanketing the region in mid and upper level clouds through Monday \nafternoon until the upper level trough moves through and brings much \ndrier air moves in and replaces the entire airmass. \n\nDespite the moisture, rain chances remain rather low across the \nregion. While the highest PWAT's will likely be Sunday, a noticeably \ndrier layer between the surface and around 800 mbs will \nsignificantly limit how much rain falls over the lowlands, with the \nmost likely outcomes ranging from virga overhead to some spotty \nlight rain accumulations. Higher chances for rain and higher precip \ntotals will occur over mountain terrain above the dry layer, though \nconvection will likely be very limited from extensive cloud cover. \n\nWhile the trough will help provide lift for showers in the deserts \n(and areas east) on Monday, models shows the troughs drier air \nmoving in just ahead of the lift from the cyclonic vorticity over \nthe rest of the region. The mountains and deserts will have the \ngreatest chances for rain Sunday (15-25%) and Monday (15-35%). \nChances stay around 10-25% both days elsewhere, generally higher \nmoving south and east across Socal.\n\nIn terms of temperatures, highs will be around 5-10 degrees above \naverage today as the ridge to the east maintains its presence for \none more day. Highs in the mid-to-upper-70s in higher mountain \ncommunities and beaches, 80s for coastal areas and lower mountain \nareas, 90s for inland valleys and Inland Empire, and around 100-105 \nin the Coachella Valley. Highs Sunday drop several degrees inland \ndue to the extensive cloud cover, then drop more significantly \nMonday, another 6-12 degrees regionwide, as the upper level trough \nmoves through. Highs slowly rebound through the rest of the week \nback to average for late September as another ridge develops and \nexpands off of the coast. As a result, coastal temperatures will be \na wild card in the late week forecast, depending on where the \nthermal through sets up.",
